From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:29:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] media: apple: vxd: decode H.264 pictures through the mailbox

device_run() now builds and submits real work:

- The decode context allocates the buffers DecodeCreateH264VLD gives the
  AVC path: the 4 KiB packed CAVLC table (CRC-checked after the copy),
  the IntraBuffer (128 KiB) and the StreamStateBuffer (8 KiB, message
  w5), zeroed and GART-mapped.  Every CAPTURE buffer gets its stored
  macroblock parameter buffer ((W * H * 0x80 + 0x41ff) & ~0xfff, BE
  0x62ec) with its own lifetime.
- The OUTPUT queue keeps a kernel mapping: in frame-based mode the
  driver walks the access unit itself.
- NV12 CAPTURE strides are rounded up to 256 bytes.  Every MSVDX surface
  base register drops the low 12 bits; with a 16-line aligned height
  that keeps the chroma plane of a single-plane NV12 buffer 4 KiB
  aligned.
- Per picture: SPS/PPS checks, the slice walk, RefPicList0 per P slice
  (v4l2-h264 helper plus the slice's modifications), the DPB resolved to
  CAPTURE device addresses by timestamp, the command stream into a
  pooled buffer, and one 0x81 message per slice.  The request's
  controls are completed before the submit.
- apple_vxd_submit() arms one fence per packet with Apple's arithmetic
  ((seq << 4) | packet * 2), so a 0xc0 for any packet is recognised
  whether the firmware answers every packet or only the 0x4004 one,
  binds the buffer to the last fence, writes 0x1008 = 0xf00 after a GART
  change as the kext does (MMU_CONTROL1 directory-cache invalidate in
  Imagination's layout), and pushes the messages in packet order.
- The last packet's 0xc0 finishes the job from a work item (the IRQ
  path only signals); a fault bit on any answered packet fails the
  picture.  A 1 s watchdog fails a picture that never completes and
  leaves its command buffer quarantined.  The old watchdog called
  job_finish(NULL, ...), which returned no buffer at all.

At most eight packets per picture this round (the fence packet index
has three bits).  The firmware never decoded a picture before this
patch, so the first device run is the experiment; see the test plan.

Signed-off-by: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <maintainer@example.invalid>
